\entrytitle{82}{Symmetric-key recovery in the ideal-cipher model}{strengthPolynomial}{evidenceProved}{hardwareExponential}
\setcounter{problem}{81}
\begin{problem}[Symmetric-key recovery in the ideal-cipher model]
Given plaintext--ciphertext pairs $(P_i,C_i)$ generated by a block cipher $E_K$ with a uniformly random $k$-bit key, find a key satisfying every equation $E_K(P_i)=C_i$. Choose sufficiently many pairs that the key is unique with high probability. The ideal-cipher comparison counts cipher-oracle queries; explicit implementations use quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity.
\end{problem}

\speedupheading
\complexity{Quantum}{$\Theta(2^{k/2})$ cipher queries, Grover key search.}
With sufficiently many known pairs to isolate the key, a reversible verification circuit of $G$ gates and workspace $W$ gives $O(2^{k/2}(G+k))$ gates and $k+W$ logical qubits, up to the registers needed for the supplied pairs. The cost includes verification and uncomputation. Constant success probability is obtained after the usual number of amplitude-amplification iterations.

\complexity{Classical}{$\Theta(2^k)$ ideal-cipher queries, exhaustive key search; explicit small circuits admit Boolean tensor contraction.}
The query bound is tight for a uniformly random key in the ideal-cipher model. If the cipher is given by an explicit verification circuit with $G$ gates, its key-recovery predicate has $O(k+G)$-size 3-CNF encoding. Tensor contraction and witness recovery give $\operatorname{poly}(k+G)2^{O((k+G)/\sqrt{\log(k+G)})}$ bit complexity. For $G=O(k)$ this is subexponential in $k$. The ideal-cipher query lower bound and the explicit-circuit upper bound refer to their respective input representations.

\dimensionlabel{Strength}{strengthPolynomial} Tight quadratic polynomial speedup in the ideal-cipher key-space size, entirely from Grover search. For $2^k$ keys it halves the exponential constant; explicit linear-size verification circuits have the stated subexponential classical comparator.

\dimensionlabel{Evidence}{evidenceProved} Fully proved in the ideal-cipher query model. For an explicit cipher, the comparison includes its best structural and circuit-based attacks.

\dimensionlabel{Hardware}{hardwareExponential} Cryptographic search requires fault tolerance and exponential coherent depth. The Grassl--Langenberg--Roetteler--Steinwandt AES-128 construction uses 2,953 logical qubits and roughly $2^{87}$ gates for full key search. A logical circuit estimate includes reversible rounds, key schedule, multi-pair checks and diffusion.

{\small [Before ``ETH is false'', the generic explicit-key-search baseline was $O(2^k)$ classical trials versus $O(2^{k/2})$ quantum trials, a quadratic speedup.]\par}
